  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nOur active pattern remains firmly in place through the weekend with \na stationary front sitting overhead. Daily rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms are expected through the weekend. A few storms could \nbe strong to severe this weekend, especially Saturday afternoon. \nMain hazards with strong to severe storms will be damaging winds, \nbut a tornado threat does exist Saturday afternoon across southeast \nOhio with a meso low creating backed winds and 20-40 knots of 0-6km \nwind shear.\n\nHeavy rainfall and flash flooding will ultimately be our main \nconcern this weekend with precipitable water remaining high between \n1.75\" and 2.50\". Efficient rainfall rates upwards of 2-4\"/hr or more \nwill be common with any showers and thunderstorms. Soils are already \nwaterlogged so flash flooding is likely where repeated rounds \nof heavy rainfall occur. Latest HRRR and NAM3k show stripes of 2\ninches or more of rainfall being picked up somewhere across the\nsouthern coalfields and northeastern Kentucky through this\nevening. CAMs, namely the FV3, do show a few hefty rounds of\nshowers and thunderstorms along and south of the I-64 corridor\nthis afternoon and tonight. \n\nA flash flood watch painted across the entire forecast area goes\nuntil tonight at 11 PM. Not inclined to extend this watch into\nSaturday as all models shove the activity out of here by\n10PM-11PM. Plus, models do not agree on solutions for tomorrow.\nThe NAM3k and HRRR keep the area less active with less\nproblematic QPF values across the area; the GFS however shows \nthe opposite. Nonetheless, WPC has our area outlooked with\nslight risks (level 2/4) across our forecast area through\nSunday. Additional flood watches may be on the horizon going\ninto Sunday, especially with CAMs showing a hefty complex\nforming across Ohio that will then move across our northern\nforecast area late Saturday into early Sunday. \n\nThe pattern looks to break some Tuesday and part of Wednesday as\nsurface high pressure builds into the area. Zonal flow aloft may\nallow for some sneaky shortwave disturbances to move through\nallowing for chances for some diurnal showers/storms across the\nmountains. The pattern then looks to turn active once again\nWednesday night/Thursday into the weekend. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nTemperatures hot on Sunday with the lowlands seeing upper 80s\nand low 90s. The mountains will remain in the upper 70s to mid\n80s. Dew points will be oppressive with the saturated air mass\nwith 70s being forecasted each afternoon across the area. This\nwill in turn lead to dangerous heat index values in the lower\n100s Sunday afternoon across portions of the lowlands. \nCurrently, these values remain below advisory criteria though so\nno headlines are expected at this time. If dew points and\ntemperatures rise with future updates, then some locations may\nbe in advisory territory.",
